1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,240
The Clinch River Breeder Reactor Project began in June 1970 when the United States Congress

2
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:29,839
authorized the Atomic Energy Commission to undertake the design, construction and operations

3
00:00:29,839 --> 00:00:34,460
of the nation's first liquid metal fast breeder reactor demonstration plant.

4
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,060
The project was established with the principal objectives of,

5
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:44,679
first, demonstrating that the liquid metal fast breeder can operate reliably and safely,

6
00:00:45,439 --> 00:00:50,320
and, second, demonstrating the economics of the fast breeder reactor cycle.

7
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,900
This is the Clinch River, Oak Ridge, Tennessee,

8
00:00:57,699 --> 00:01:00,500
future site of the Clinch River Fast Breeder reactor.

9
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:06,500
This 1364 acre site is owned by the United States government

10
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,900
and is managed by the Tennessee Valley Authority.

11
00:01:11,459 --> 00:01:16,500
The Clinch River Reactor is a sodium-cooled, fast breeder nuclear power plant

12
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:20,819
designed for a net electrical output of 350 megawatts.

13
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:39,680
The major systems of the plant are the reactor, heat transport, steam generator and feed water systems, turbine generator, fuel

14
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:44,520
handling system and various auxiliary systems required to support plant

15
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:50,080
operation and maintenance. In a joint effort by government, utilities and

16
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:54,520
industry, the design, development and fabrication of the plant and its

17
00:01:54,519 --> 00:02:00,099
components are well underway. Project participants from industry include the

18
00:02:00,099 --> 00:02:03,780
Atomics International Division of the Rockwell International Energy Systems

19
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:11,639
Group, Westinghouse, General Electric, Burns and Rowe, and Stone and Webster. One

20
00:02:11,639 --> 00:02:15,519
of Atomics International's responsibilities is the design and

21
00:02:15,519 --> 00:02:21,400
procurement of the reactor fuel handling system and associated components. The

22
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:26,800
The reactor system requires periodic refueling, nominally once a year to replenish the supply

23
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,580
of fuel.

24
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:33,140
The fuel handling system provides the facilities and equipment necessary to accomplish the

25
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:39,000
yearly refueling operations and all associated functions, such as fuel handling within the

26
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,920
reactor vessel, fuel handling and storage outside the vessel, and shipping and receiving

27
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:48,460
operations on site.

28
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:54,060
handling operations take place in two buildings the containment building which houses the reactor

29
00:02:54,780 --> 00:03:00,060
and the service building which contains fuel handling facilities and ancillary equipment

30
00:03:01,819 --> 00:03:07,340
equipment and facilities located in the reactor containment building are limited to those involved

31
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:13,420
with fuel handling inside the reactor during shutdown they include the in-vessel transfer

32
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:19,900
machine referred to as IVTM for moving fuel within the reactor vessel and the

33
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:28,000
auxiliary handling machine or AHM for installing and removing the IVTM. The

34
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,220
IVTM is mounted on the smallest of the triple rotatable plugs which form the

35
00:03:33,220 --> 00:03:41,420
closure head of the reactor. By rotating the three plugs the IVTM can be located

36
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:43,680
over any core position in the reactor.

37
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:51,080
The IVTM can then withdraw a spent assembly from the core

38
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:55,800
or insert a new assembly by vertical motions and grappling action.

39
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,180
Lateral movement of the core assembly within the reactor vessel

40
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:07,160
is accomplished by translation of the rotatable flux.

41
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:26,580
The majority of the fuel handling equipment and facilities is in the reactor service building,

42
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:31,360
which is adjacent to the reactor containment building.

43
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:36,720
Fuel handling facilities and equipment located in the service building include a sodium-filled

44
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:42,500
storage tank for temporary storage of spent fuel, a fuel handling cell for inspection

45
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:48,820
and handling of spent fuel assemblies, and facilities for receiving new fuel and for

46
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:52,300
shipping spent fuel.

47
00:04:52,300 --> 00:05:01,340
The X-Vessel Storage Tank, or EZST, is a 20 foot diameter by 45 foot deep sodium vessel.

48
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:07,520
contains a two-tier rotatable turntable capable of holding 650 fuel assemblies.

49
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:12,340
The assemblies are stored in sodium-filled pots with two pots stacked

50
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:18,620
one above the other in each storage tube. Access to the storage positions is

51
00:05:18,620 --> 00:05:23,840
obtained through nine openings in the closure head. The turntable is rotated to

52
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:30,260
gain access to each storage position in each row. The storage tank also contains

53
00:05:30,259 --> 00:05:36,019
a number of dry argon gas-filled preheat tubes located in the upper tiers where

54
00:05:36,019 --> 00:05:39,980
cold assemblies are placed for preheating prior to immersion in the hot

55
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:46,659
sodium. The fuel handling cell is a small hot cell with three stations, a

56
00:05:46,659 --> 00:05:53,659
maintenance station, a fuel examination station, and a spent fuel loading station.

57
00:05:53,659 --> 00:05:58,339
Selected fuel assemblies can be visually inspected and dimensionally measured in

58
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:06,100
this facility. In addition, spent fuel assemblies are prepared for shipment off-site. Prior to loading

59
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:11,380
operations, a shipping cask is positioned and sealed below an opening in the floor of the cell.

60
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:16,500
The fuel assembly can now be transferred to the cask through the floor opening.

61
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:23,860
In addition to the equipment and facilities located in the containment and service buildings,

62
00:06:24,660 --> 00:06:30,740
fuel movement between buildings and facilities is accomplished using the Ex-vessel Transfer Machine,

63
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:36,340
or EVTM, a heavily shielded and cooled transfer machine mounted on a gantry.

64
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:44,740
The EVTM has a central eight inch diameter cavity for carrying a fuel assembly. This cavity

65
00:06:44,740 --> 00:06:50,819
contains an argon atmosphere. Due to their negligible heat production new fuel assemblies

66
00:06:50,819 --> 00:06:55,680
are handled bare, but spent fuel must be carried in sodium filled pots to

67
00:06:55,680 --> 00:07:02,939
adequately cool the fuel assembly. The sequence for handling fuel begins with

68
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:08,459
the arrival of new fuel on site in lightly shielded containers. Once the

69
00:07:08,459 --> 00:07:12,620
containers and their contents have been checked, a shipping container is placed

70
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:16,459
in one of the two new fuel unloading stations by the service building crane.

71
00:07:21,819 --> 00:07:25,579
Here the shipping container atmosphere is changed from air to argon.

72
00:07:26,939 --> 00:07:32,540
After the container has been inerted, the EVTM picks up the assembly and transfers it to a

73
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:39,259
special preheat station in the EDST where it is preheated in argon to 400 degrees Fahrenheit.

74
00:07:39,259 --> 00:07:46,199
After preheat is complete, the assembly is transferred by the EVPM from the preheat

75
00:07:46,199 --> 00:07:53,060
station to a sodium-filled pot in one of the storage positions in the EVST. The fuel remains

76
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:58,519
in this position until the reactor is shut down and made ready for replacement of fuel assemblies.

77
00:07:58,519 --> 00:08:05,719
After reactor shutdown, refueling preparations begin by removal of the large diameter equipment

78
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:12,300
hatch to allow movement between the containment and service building. Also, the IVTM is then

79
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:16,780
installed in the reactor head by means of the AHM and the building crane.

80
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:43,460
Floor valves are positioned over access openings to the reactor and the ex-vessel storage tank.

81
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:47,480
The reactor is now ready to be refueled.

82
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:53,120
The IVTM, in conjunction with the rotatable plug, removes the spent fuel assembly from

83
00:08:53,120 --> 00:09:00,080
the core and deposits it in an empty pot located in a transfer position outside the core but

84
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:02,800
still within the reactor vessel.

85
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:09,519
At the same time, the EVTM removes the new fuel assembly from the EVST and transports

86
00:09:09,519 --> 00:09:12,079
the assembly to the reactor.

87
00:09:12,079 --> 00:09:18,359
By sealing the EVTM to a special port in the head of the reactor, the EVTM can lower the

88
00:09:18,359 --> 00:09:25,539
new fuel assembly into a second transfer position in the reactor, within reach of the IVTM.

89
00:09:25,539 --> 00:09:31,120
During the same attachment operation to the reactor head, the EVTM removes the spent fuel

90
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:35,120
assembly in the first transfer position.

91
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:42,820
While the EVTM transports the spent fuel to the storage vessel, the IVTM

92
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:47,200
completes the cycle by installing the new fuel assembly into the open core

93
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:56,440
lattice. These operations are repeated until the reactor core has been refueled.

94
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:03,040
When refueling is completed, the reactor is brought back online. After the spent

95
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:08,280
fuel in the storage tank has decayed for approximately 100 days, the assemblies

96
00:10:08,280 --> 00:10:14,260
are ready to be shipped off-site. A fuel assembly is transferred by the EVTM in a

97
00:10:14,260 --> 00:10:20,780
sodium-filled pot from the storage tank to the fuel handling cell.

98
00:10:26,440 --> 00:10:32,900
The fuel assembly is removed from the pot by means of a gas-cooled grapple for loading into the shipping cask.

99
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:39,060
Capabilities exist in the cell to visually and dimensionally inspect the assembly.

100
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:44,840
A spent fuel shipping cask is brought into the service building by a special railroad car,

101
00:10:45,700 --> 00:10:50,700
moved to a position beneath the fuel handling cell, and sealed to the loading port.

102
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:55,040
Fuel can now be transferred from the cell to the shipping cask.

103
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:02,840
When the cask is full, it is disconnected from the fuel handling cells, inspected for

104
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:08,600
conformance to shipping regulations, and then transported by a rail to a fuel reprocess.

105
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:16,200
The Quench River's fuel handling system is well on its way to becoming a reality.

106
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:23,240
The necessary development testing has been complete, as has most of the engineering design.

107
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:34,000
Many of the major fuel handling components are being fabricated and some are presently being proof tested.

108
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:44,840
The IVTM was designed and fabricated by Atomics International at Canoga Park, California.

109
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:53,860
Component tests were conducted between 1974 and 1977 to support the IVTM design phase.

110
00:11:53,860 --> 00:12:02,220
These tests included environmental testing of the grapple drive rod seals, performance testing of

111
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:08,800
the drive motor, braking system and gear trains, and kinematic testing of the grapple under adverse

112
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:17,240
conditions. Manufacturing began in August of 1977 with machining and assembly of the 43-foot long

113
00:12:17,240 --> 00:12:25,039
in-vessel sub-assembly on the grapple. A drive tower structure was fabricated to

114
00:12:25,039 --> 00:12:30,680
house the grapple actuator mechanism. Assembly of pneumatic and electrical

115
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,460
components on the equipment bay structure completed the last major

116
00:12:34,460 --> 00:12:45,480
sub assembly. In May of 1979, the major sub-assemblies were delivered to the

117
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:50,960
the Energy Technology and Engineering Center located at Rockwell International's Santa

118
00:12:50,960 --> 00:12:59,320
Susana Field Laboratory. Here, the IVTM was assembled into a 65-foot high machine to undergo

119
00:12:59,320 --> 00:13:08,980
a testing program which would be equivalent to five complete core refuelers. The testing

120
00:13:08,980 --> 00:13:16,320
program was divided into two stages. The first stage, completed in July of 1979, was a dry

121
00:13:16,320 --> 00:13:22,680
checkout to verify the IVTM's capability to withdraw and insert the core assemblies through

122
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:28,360
the full range of misalignments and loads expected during refueling operations. The

123
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:34,240
second stage, performance testing the IVTM in a hot sodium environment, will begin in

124
00:13:34,240 --> 00:13:40,480
November of 1979. During this stage the IVTM computer controls will be checked

125
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:46,320
out concurrently with the testing of the machines. After the IVTM has completed

126
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:51,460
its performance test, it will undergo any necessary refurbishment and then will be

127
00:13:51,460 --> 00:13:58,960
shipped to the Oak Ridge site. After extensive developmental testing of key

128
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:05,120
features of the EVTM, final design was completed and procurement was initiated.

129
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:12,280
In 1973 and 1974, several small-scale tests investigated important heat

130
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:18,420
transfer parameters. These tests supported preliminary design decisions.

131
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:26,920
And in 1975, a full-scale test with a cooling system, cast body internals, and

132
00:14:26,920 --> 00:14:31,560
and an electrically simulated fuel assembly confirmed the final design.

133
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:38,180
Currently, all long-lead forgings and castings for the EVTM are on hand,

134
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:44,320
including the grapple drive housing and cover, upper support module, viewport housing,

135
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:49,380
drift pan housing, closure valve body, and other major modules.

136
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:54,100
Once the remaining EVTM components have been manufactured

137
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:57,340
and the grapple and grapple drive proof test completed,

138
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,820
these sub-assemblies are scheduled to be shipped to a test site in Oak Ridge

139
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:05,480
for final performance testing of the full assembled machines.

140
00:15:06,879 --> 00:15:12,019
The EVST, which is used for the storage of fuel assemblies, is well into fabrication.

141
00:15:12,980 --> 00:15:17,000
The storage vessel and components were designed by Atomics International

142
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,700
and subcontracted to a wide range of industrial concerns for fabrication.

143
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:28,740
Four forgings were procured and delivered to Babcock and Wilcox, who will fabricate the major components.

144
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:37,259
The storage vessel support plan required one of the largest forgings made by Lavish Company at their Wisconsin plant.

145
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:47,980
Starting with a 336,000 pound heated steel engine, the general shape was formed by ring forging machines and then quenched in a water pool.

146
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:52,659
followed by tempering the steel in a heat-treating furnace.

147
00:15:53,960 --> 00:15:57,600
The forging was then rough-machined to its 24-foot diameter

148
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:02,019
and delivered to Babcock and Wilcox's Mount Vernon, Indiana plant

149
00:16:02,019 --> 00:16:03,899
for final machining and assembly.

150
00:16:04,820 --> 00:16:10,300
At the Babcock and Wilcox plant, other EVST parts have been fabricated and assembled.

151
00:16:12,039 --> 00:16:15,039
Steel plates have been rolled and welded into shell cores.

152
00:16:15,039 --> 00:16:23,799
The vessel bottom head has been formed from smaller shaped pieces and final machined to

153
00:16:23,799 --> 00:16:26,379
a smooth dished head.

154
00:16:26,379 --> 00:16:33,539
The turntable grid plates for support of the 330 storage tubes have been drilled and machined

155
00:16:33,539 --> 00:16:40,099
and the vessel closure head is presently being final machined.

156
00:16:40,099 --> 00:16:44,699
Other EVST components are being fabricated by industry.

157
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:48,720
completion of these components they will be assembled with the Babcock and

158
00:16:48,720 --> 00:16:53,379
Wilcox vessels for a final functional proof test before delivery of the

159
00:16:53,379 --> 00:16:57,640
completed vessel to the Clink River site.

160
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:06,380
The fuel handling cell components are in various stages of design and development.

161
00:17:06,380 --> 00:17:12,880
The in-cell crane, Howard manipulator and the master slave manipulators have

162
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:17,700
completed fabrication and have been tested at the Santa Susana Laboratories.

163
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:23,740
The equipment performed satisfactorily under mock-up situations. In the future,

164
00:17:23,740 --> 00:17:27,720
these components will be assembled in a mock-up structure of the handling cell

165
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:32,360
of Santa Susana. There they will be used to confirm the operation of the other

166
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:37,480
shell equipment. Several mock-up components will be built and tested to

167
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:43,880
provide design information before fabrication of plant hardware. After

168
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:47,980
equipment has been checked out, the mock-up will be disassembled and shipped

169
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:55,200
to the Oak Ridge site as an operator training device. The individual fuel

170
00:17:55,200 --> 00:18:00,819
handling equipment must be controlled as an integrated system. To do this, an

171
00:18:00,819 --> 00:18:06,160
overall refueling control system has been designed, which includes three

172
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:12,800
inter-communicating mini computer. These computers have been procured and the software program has

173
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:19,279
been designed to enable the computers to perform their individual and combined functions. Tests

174
00:18:19,279 --> 00:18:23,200
are currently underway to demonstrate their overall performance capabilities.

175
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:31,279
Over 80 percent of the fuel handling equipment has been designed and most of the major components are

176
00:18:31,279 --> 00:18:38,799
in the process of fabrication. Components fully designed and ready for fabrication include the AHM,

177
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:51,680
floor valves and adapters, the EZTM gantry, the fuel handling cell maintenance and examination

178
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:58,559
equipment, and miscellaneous storage facilities. Progress has been substantial.

179
00:18:59,259 --> 00:19:03,399
Through extensive component testing to support the design phase,

180
00:19:03,759 --> 00:19:12,779
followed by confirmatory proof testing of plant hardware, fuel handling system components are verified to be safe and reliable before delivery to the plant.

181
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,740
The Clinch River components are well on their way toward completing the project.
